1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,408
This block contains 256 addresses. We want
four separate networks, with

2
00:00:05,408 --> 00:00:10,817
no gaps and no overlap. How can a few extra
bits

3
00:00:10,817 --> 00:00:16,225
create all four boundaries? Watch what happens
when we move one

4
00:00:16,225 --> 00:00:21,633
dividing line.

5
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:26,717
An IPv4 address contains thirty-two bits,
displayed as four decimal octets.

6
00:00:26,717 --> 00:00:31,800
Each octet represents eight bits. The familiar
dotted form makes the

7
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:36,883
number readable, but the block structure
becomes much clearer when we

8
00:00:36,883 --> 00:00:41,967
expose the bits underneath.

9
00:00:41,967 --> 00:00:47,367
Slash twenty-four fixes the first twenty-four
positions. The last eight may

10
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:52,767
vary, so every address in this block shares
the same beginning.

11
00:00:52,767 --> 00:00:58,167
A prefix describes that common part and tells
us which remaining

12
00:00:58,167 --> 00:01:03,567
combinations belong together.

13
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:08,542
A mask makes this boundary explicit: ones mark
the fixed prefix

14
00:01:08,542 --> 00:01:13,517
positions; zeros mark the changing positions.
For slash twenty-four, the mask

15
00:01:13,517 --> 00:01:18,492
is 255.255.255.0. The decimal form is simply
another view of those

16
00:01:18,492 --> 00:01:23,467
thirty-two bits.

17
00:01:23,467 --> 00:01:30,678
To extract the network, apply bitwise AND. A
mask one keeps

18
00:01:30,678 --> 00:01:37,889
the address bit; a zero clears it. Applying
this mask to

19
00:01:37,889 --> 00:01:45,100
192.168.1.42 leaves 192.168.1.0. The variable
part disappears, exposing the common prefix.

20
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:49,967
Eight variable bits allow two to the power of
eight combinations:

21
00:01:49,967 --> 00:01:54,833
256 addresses. In the last octet, that's zero
through 255. This

22
00:01:54,833 --> 00:01:59,700
counts every address in the block, before
assigning any special network

23
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:04,567
or broadcast roles.

24
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:09,542
Fix one more bit and the possibilities split
in half. Fix

25
00:02:09,542 --> 00:02:14,517
a second and each half splits again. Four
subnets require two

26
00:02:14,517 --> 00:02:19,492
extra prefix bits, moving our boundary from
slash twenty-four to slash

27
00:02:19,492 --> 00:02:24,467
twenty-six.

28
00:02:24,467 --> 00:02:29,550
The last octet of the new mask begins with two
ones.

29
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:34,633
Their values are 128 and sixty-four, totaling
192. Six zeros follow.

30
00:02:34,633 --> 00:02:39,717
That's why the complete slash-twenty-six mask
becomes 255.255.255.192, rather than an

31
00:02:39,717 --> 00:02:44,800
arbitrary number to memorize.

32
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:49,883
Six bits remain free inside each subnet. They
produce sixty-four addresses.

33
00:02:49,883 --> 00:02:54,967
Four times sixty-four gives the original 256,
which is a useful

34
00:02:54,967 --> 00:03:00,050
consistency check: moving the boundary divides
the existing space without changing

35
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:05,133
its total size.

36
00:03:05,133 --> 00:03:10,425
The two new subnet bits can be zero-zero,
zero-one, one-zero, or

37
00:03:10,425 --> 00:03:15,717
one-one. With all host bits cleared, those
prefixes begin at zero,

38
00:03:15,717 --> 00:03:21,008
sixty-four, 128, and 192. Four binary choices
create four evenly spaced

39
00:03:21,008 --> 00:03:26,300
boundaries.

40
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:32,933
The first subnet covers last-octet values zero
through sixty-three. On an

41
00:03:32,933 --> 00:03:39,567
ordinary LAN, zero identifies the network and
sixty-three is broadcast. Hosts

42
00:03:39,567 --> 00:03:46,200
use one through sixty-two: sixty-two usable
addresses within a sixty-four-address block.

43
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:51,608
The second subnet starts at sixty-four and
ends at 127. Hosts

44
00:03:51,608 --> 00:03:57,017
occupy sixty-five through 126. Notice the
broadcast ends in 127 here.

45
00:03:57,017 --> 00:04:02,425
Its identity comes from all host bits being
one, not from

46
00:04:02,425 --> 00:04:07,833
always ending in 255.

47
00:04:07,833 --> 00:04:13,025
The third prefix begins at 128 and covers
through 191. Its

48
00:04:13,025 --> 00:04:18,217
ordinary host range is 129 through 190. The
calculation hasn't changed;

49
00:04:18,217 --> 00:04:23,408
the fixed subnet bits have moved us into the
next group

50
00:04:23,408 --> 00:04:28,600
of sixty-four combinations.

51
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:33,683
The fourth block runs from 192 through 255,
with hosts from

52
00:04:33,683 --> 00:04:38,767
193 through 254. Together, the four ranges
cover the parent exactly

53
00:04:38,767 --> 00:04:43,850
once. Their boundaries meet cleanly, and each
address belongs to just

54
00:04:43,850 --> 00:04:48,933
one subnet.

55
00:04:48,933 --> 00:04:55,133
Where does seventy fit? Its binary last octet
is 01000110. AND

56
00:04:55,133 --> 00:05:01,333
with 11000000 leaves 01000000: sixty-four. So
192.168.1.70 lies in 192.168.1.64 slash

57
00:05:01,333 --> 00:05:07,533
twenty-six. The mask finds the block directly,
without searching a table.

58
00:05:07,533 --> 00:05:12,725
Seventy and one hundred share that sixty-four
prefix. Address 130 belongs

59
00:05:12,725 --> 00:05:17,917
to the block starting at 128. Similar decimal
addresses can cross

60
00:05:17,917 --> 00:05:23,108
a subnet boundary; masking both addresses
reveals whether their network portions

61
00:05:23,108 --> 00:05:28,300
actually match.

62
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:33,700
That boundary affects delivery. A host
normally reaches a destination in

63
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:39,100
its own subnet locally; another subnet needs a
suitable next-hop router.

64
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:44,500
This is a logical addressing decision,
independent of whether the devices

65
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:49,900
occupy different physical rooms.

66
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:55,200
Suppose the network needs fifty host
addresses. Slash twenty-seven offers thirty

67
00:05:55,200 --> 00:06:00,500
usable addresses on an ordinary LAN, which
isn't enough. Slash twenty-six

68
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:05,800
offers sixty-two. Work backward from the
required capacity to choose the

69
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:11,100
smallest suitable block.

70
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:18,311
Every extra fixed prefix bit halves the
remaining combinations. Slash twenty-five

71
00:06:18,311 --> 00:06:25,522
gives 128 total addresses, slash twenty-six
sixty-four, slash twenty-seven thirty-two.
Longer

72
00:06:25,522 --> 00:06:32,733
prefixes mean smaller blocks because fewer
positions remain free to change.

73
00:06:32,733 --> 00:06:37,925
Subtracting two is useful for ordinary LAN
subnets, but isn't universal.

74
00:06:37,925 --> 00:06:43,117
Suitable slash-thirty-one point-to-point links
can use both addresses; slash thirty-two
identifies

75
00:06:43,117 --> 00:06:48,308
a single address. The address roles depend on
the network's purpose

76
00:06:48,308 --> 00:06:53,500
and prefix.

77
00:06:53,500 --> 00:07:00,856
A subnet boundary doesn't itself encrypt
traffic or forbid crossing. Routing

78
00:07:00,856 --> 00:07:08,211
and access policies determine which
communication is allowed. Splitting an address

79
00:07:08,211 --> 00:07:15,567
block organizes delivery; protection requires
the relevant security mechanisms as well.

80
00:07:15,567 --> 00:07:21,075
Modern classless addressing uses the supplied
prefix, rather than guessing a

81
00:07:21,075 --> 00:07:26,583
class from the first octet. Also, our
192.168.1 block uses private

82
00:07:26,583 --> 00:07:32,092
addresses. It illustrates internal network
organization, rather than a globally routable

83
00:07:32,092 --> 00:07:37,600
public block.

84
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:42,575
Check the four starts: zero, sixty-four, 128,
192. Each broadcast sits

85
00:07:42,575 --> 00:07:47,550
one below the next boundary, ending at 255 for
the last

86
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:52,525
block. Equal block sizes and clean boundaries
let us verify the

87
00:07:52,525 --> 00:07:57,500
entire split together.

88
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:03,125
Two bits created four prefixes; six remaining
bits created sixty-four addresses

89
00:08:03,125 --> 00:08:08,750
per prefix. Masks, ranges, and host counts all
follow from that

90
00:08:08,750 --> 00:08:14,375
same boundary. Once you see the division, the
separate-looking rules become

91
00:08:14,375 --> 00:08:20,000
consequences of one picture.
